{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/478979-help-building-a-necropolis","id":478979,"title":"Help building a Necropolis","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-04-13T05:48:39-07:00","id":478979,"title":"Help building a Necropolis","content":"<span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">Hi,</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">I'm trying to build a Necropolis and was wondering if anyone has any advice on doing so. Things like ranks, positions and roles that are generally used in a Necropolis. </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">And any advice in general for Wraith itself.<br /> <br /> I'm setting my game here</span></span><br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eureka_Rebellion\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Eureka_stockade_battle.jpg/1280px-Eureka_stockade_battle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=index&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eureka_Rebellion\" target=\"_blank\"> Eureka Rebellion - Wikipedia </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eureka_Rebellion\" target=\"_blank\"> http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eureka_Rebellion </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> So I've got a tonne of Heirarchy vs Renegade stuff to work with. I just want to know if Wraith has analogues to Sheriff, Harpy etc<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">Thanks.</span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5675-jivewookiee","name":"Jivewookiee","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-13T22:06:49-07:00","id":479911,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:courier new\">The first thing I do when pulling a Necropolis together is figure out the political climates. I know it sounds a little boring, but it really helps steer the types of stories your troupe wants to tell.<br /> <br /> Are the PCs going to belong to one faction over another?<br /> What faction, if any, has control of the area? Or the most control? Or does anyone have control and are there just a few strong Haunts holding little fiefdoms?<br /> <br /> Also, are you setting the game in the area of the Eureka Rebellion, but in the modern era? Or is it set *during* the Eureka Rebellion? Not that it matters; just curious.<br /> <br /> As for parallels to VtMs Sheriff, Harpy (et al), I think Hierarchy ranks (military and civilian) might fill those particular voids. In Heretic and Renegade territory, you could always make up regional titles.<br /> <br /> [General Advice: If it\u2019s your troupe\u2019s first Wraith game, don\u2019t be afraid to ignore Shadowguiding. I don\u2019t encourage ignoring it because it\u2019s a wonderfully entertaining facet of the game, but I have seen a few new players get weirded out by the idea.]<br /> <br /> Hope this helps get the synaptic juices flowing.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:8px\"><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">&lt;signature&gt; something witty &lt;/signature&gt;</span></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/302-nazfool","name":"Nazfool","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar302_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"753"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-04-14T01:50:56-07:00","id":480012,"title":"","content":"The Wraith Project produced a fan sourcebook for building Necropoli. It's actually pretty useful, and I've used it myself: <a href=\"http://www.cattail.nu/wraithproject/docs/bodeadcities.DOC\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.cattail.nu/wraithproject/...deadcities.DOC</a><br /> <br /> Following on from Nazfool's suggestion about possibly dropping Shadowguiding for new teams, I have a suggestion too. Basically, I've occasionally swapped the rules for Shadowguiding and the P'o from Wraith and Kindred of the East to alter the tone of either game. This might be for just a session, or for an entire story.<br /> <br /> So, if your troupe is new, how about pinching KotE's way of dealing with the dark half of the soul? Rather than have people Shadowguiding each other's Shadows, you simply make it more abstract, replacing the inner chatter with a few resisted rolls of Willpower versus Angst at opportune moments (exposure to dark emotions, moments when characters might feel conflicted, encounters with spectres). However, the object here isn't Catharsis, but rather confusion, undermining the Psyche's choices and representing the Shadow's tempting.<br /> <br /> If the Shadow wins the resisted roll, for example, the Storyteller might rule that the wraith's next action might be influenced by the Shadow (either changing the action partially to suit the Shadow's agenda, or adding or removing dice from one roll equal to the net successes). Alternatively, success might just mean the wraith receives an intrusive thought or memory. The Storyteller should do this sparingly, but at just the right moments.<br /> <br /> This can make the path more treacherous for wraiths, without having to delve too deeply into the psychological aspect of Shadowguiding, or the adversarial element it can add between players. That is often the hardest part for young or inexperienced players, as roleplaying the Shadow can be a harrowing (lower case) experience.<br /> <br /> Likewise, Harrowings can be abstracted in the same way: use contested rolls to see if the wraith succeeds or not. Either side might need a fixed number of successes over several turns to win, or you might just call for one roll with a variable difficulty. <br /> <br /> This can speed up play, but of course takes away from the players' opportunities to roleplay their way out of the Harrowing. It also makes loss of a quarry entirely dependent on dice rolls, rather than decision-making. To balance that out, you might just frame one to three questions/riddles/suppositions for the player to answer. These should be about the wraith and the reasons she fell into the Harrowing in general (what did your wraith do wrong here? How can she avoid this in future? What would she learn from this experience?) A correct/meaningful/insightful answer might translate to an automatic success.<br /> <br /> Good luck!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}